      isoFaq1Q: "How does blocking images safeguard my location and identity?",
      isoFaq1A: "Tracking pixels are microscopic images hosted on marketing servers. When your browser requests that image, the server logs your IP address and physical location. Blocking the request prevents this surveillance entirely.",
      isoFaq2Q: "Can emails be recovered after the 20-minute countdown ends?",
      isoFaq2A: "No. Once the timer reaches zero or you click Burn Mailbox, all container keys are discarded and memory is permanently cleared.",
      isoFaq3Q: "Do you log my IP address or set tracking cookies?",
      isoFaq3A: "No. We operate under a strict zero-logs policy and use no marketing, analytical, or profiling cookies whatsoever.",
      mlBadge: "Authentication Engineering",
      mlTitle: "Magic Links vs. OTP Verification Codes: How Our Passive Engine Preserves Single-Use Tokens",
      mlSubtitle: "An architectural breakdown of why passwordless login links burn prematurely on legacy disposable mail platforms and how our passive ingestion engine preserves tokens intact.",
      mlHighlight1: "Protects single-use magic login links from premature expiration caused by pre-fetch web crawlers.",
      mlHighlight2: "Passive HTML ingestion ensures no automated GET requests are fired against your confirmation URLs.",
      mlHighlight3: "Direct link extraction surfaces the target destination cleanly with one-click copy capability.",
      mlHighlight4: "Fully compatible with modern passwordless stacks including Supabase, Auth0, and Firebase.",
      mlSec1Title: "The Dilemma: Why Magic Links Expire Prematurely on Other Services",
      mlSec1P1: "Passwordless authentication relies on single-use encrypted URLs. When an authentication provider sends a magic link, legacy temporary email platforms often run automated link scanners that fire preview requests against every link in the email.",
      mlSec1P2: "The moment an automated crawler touches a single-use URL, the token is consumed. When the human user clicks the link moments later, they are greeted by a frustrating error: \"This magic link has already been used or expired.\"",
      mlSec2Title: "How Our Passive Ingestion Engine Protects Your Tokens",
      mlSec2P1: "We engineered our mail parser to be completely passive. We inspect inbound HTML payloads without executing network requests or pre-fetching external endpoints.",
      mlSec2P2: "The verified link is extracted, stripped of unnecessary redirect parameters, and delivered straight to your screen, ensuring that you—and only you—initiate the single-use authentication request.",
      mlCompRow1Feat: "Single-Use Token Handling",
